ore this wasn't a  partisan issue this was just a a  practical on the ground type of of issue  and so hopefully the administration's  going back to that kind of perspective  and take the politics out of it and just  get what's needed  for the border are they doing enough mr  mayor because in yuma arizona you are in  that sector the third busiest in the  nation you've had 35 migrant deaths this  fiscal year a stat not made public by  border patrol but one that i have uh  from my border patrol sources and yet it  doesn't seem that it's getting any  better  and actually i think those numbers are  even higher because that doesn't include  like we had a couple people pass away  within the city limits which this police  department responded to instead of  border patrol so it is definitely a huge  issue and  having  something that is going in the right  direction is great it is a first step  there are many more steps that need to  get done but without this first step  we'll never get to the other one so this  is a  movement in the right direction um  arizona has encountered nearly 265  000 migrants this fiscal year three more  months to go but here in washington  where i am our mayor d.c mayor muriel  bowser is calling it a humanitarian  crisis after receiving just 4 000  migrants here in the nation's capitol  here's some of what she had to say about  calling in the national guard listen  we need space uh and we we need the  federal government to be involved so  i've asked for the deployment of the  guard as long as we need the guard to  deal with the humanitarian crisis that  we expect to escalate the number of  people crossing the border seeking  asylum we expect to only go up  what do you make of it  well i think it's great they're joining  conversation this is something we've  been talking about for over a year and a  half and to finally have these other  mayors that understand are beginning to  understand what that  impact is you know  washington dc is millions of people  and they're concerned about 4 000 people  we get over 5 000 people in a week  through the yuma area and we're only a  community of 100 000 people so if  they're finally getting to the  understanding that uh this is a bigger  issue than just for the little towns  along the border i think it's a great  thing  yeah and you can see here on our screen  these are migrants that have been  dropped off outside of union station  sleeping there it's a very sad  humanitarian crisis indeed the mayor's  right and she's pointing to the fact  that this is a federal  responsibility do you believe this  administration is abdicating that  responsibility  well i believe they are but it's not in  washington d.c they are doing that  actually at the border when they they  facilitate the processing of people to  come in to wait the five to six years  for a judge  there needs to be a change that that  stops that process otherwise all we're  doing is continuing the process of  trafficking that the cartels started  so the federal government needs to find  ways policies  different just enforcing the laws that  are on the books would then help stop  that traffic from happening and end up  in a situation like in washington dc in  just in the 30 seconds or so we've got  mr mayor the other thing that the  cartels are taking advantage of an open  border is pushing  drugs particularly the deadly drug  fentanyl across what are you seeing in  the hume area well we're since border  patrol is finally able to get back to  some of the border protection activity  we're actually seeing an increase in the  number of apprehensions of smugglers as  well as  actual drugs being caught but there's  still a dramatic number of of uh traffic  amount of trafficking that is probably  not getting caught and still getting  into the interior of the united states a  lot we don't know about more than 500  000 known got aways that's a story for  another day and a concerning one indeed  mr mayor thank you for taking tim